# Fetch the worker-name roster, once per firing.
#
# **This exists because the unit file has no supervisor loop and `run-pool.sh`
# does.** That script folds the refresh into a background `sh` loop and explains
# why in its own comment: the box it was written for had no cron and was denied
# `loginctl enable-linger`, so the two documented schedulers were a cron that did
# not exist and a timer that could not run. Neither is true everywhere -- on a
# host where lingering *is* granted the timer is the better answer, because
# nothing is supervising `glados-pool.service` except systemd and a refresh
# living inside a script that is not running refreshes nothing.
#
# So the two arrangements are alternatives rather than a duplication: run the
# script, or run the unit plus this timer. Running both would have two writers on
# one file, which the `mv` makes safe and which is still nobody's intent.
#
# **Without either, `--roster` is decorative.** The pool re-reads the file every
# thirty seconds and nothing downloads it, so an absent roster means every worker
# name is admitted -- which the pool says at startup, in those words, and which is
# fail-open by design rather than by omission. Read that line before believing the
# flag is doing anything.
[Unit]
Description=Refresh the GLaDOS pool's worker roster
Documentation=https://github.com/IlumCI/GLaDOS/blob/main/supabase/README.md
After=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
StateDirectory=glados-pool

# **The `.tmp` and the `mv` are the whole of the correctness here**, and the
# reasoning is `design/runbook.md`'s: a rename within one filesystem is atomic, so
# the pool never parses a half-written document. Writing straight to the file
# would hand it a truncated one -- and although a parse failure leaves the
# previous roster in place, that is a safety net and not a plan.
#
# `--fail` matters as much: without it curl writes a 404 body to the file and
# exits 0, so the roster becomes an error page that parses as neither JSON nor a
# mapping. `-fsS` keeps it quiet on success and loud on failure, which is what a
# oneshot in a journal wants.
#
# A failed fetch leaves the last good roster alone and is not retried harder. The
# pool fails open on an absent one either way, so the honest outcome is a stale
# roster that says so in the journal rather than a unit that thrashes.
ExecStart=/usr/bin/curl -fsS --max-time 30 \
    https://vermcdgpqncfsralpesz.supabase.co/functions/v1/worker/map \
    -o %S/glados-pool/roster.json.tmp
ExecStart=/usr/bin/mv -f %S/glados-pool/roster.json.tmp %S/glados-pool/roster.json

NoNewPrivileges=true
RestrictRealtime=true
RestrictSUIDSGID=true
LockPersonality=true
SystemCallArchitectures=native
